    {
      "id": "0q27lThIk0RAlUKogaYvP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -356.10098693453386,
      "y": 181.660783690025,
      "width": 609.1813169883906,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1577733132,
      "version": 2007,
      "versionNonce": 1220579468,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783052192761,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "OK, so essentially the idea is that we have we only compare\ntwo words at a time and that would be like a certain word\nand the next word in the ray so you're just comparing\nadjacent words here and the idea is that you build adjacent\nseas right build adjacency list so you can only determine one\nadjacency based on one comparison so one comparison means\nyou at one thing one piece of information you cannot do\nanything after that single adjacency because everything after\nthat is entirely like indeducible so you basically look at the\ntwo words then you have pointers to both of the words like\na certain character starting from the beginning and you just\nkeep advancing them until the letter is different at that\npoint you simply point the letter of the first word to the\nsecond word in the graph as the adjacency there and then\nyou just keep doing that for every single pair adjacent in the\nlist and by that point you would have a graph that\nessentially you know has a bunch of adjacencies and it might\nhappen that they overlap a lot and so you would perform a\ntopological sort on that final graph and then I guess as to\nthe fact that there could be invalid dictionaries is that\nmaybe a topological sort is not possible in the graph, so\nexample, maybe there's a cycle in the graph right? I think that\nwould make the most sense if there's a cycle in the graph it's\nnot possible and that's when you would return false or the\nempty string. I think that is the core of this problem and I\nthink I actually should just do it like that should be pretty\nsimple. I don't know, though let's see.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "OK, so essentially the idea is that we have we only compare two words at a time and that would be like a certain word and the next word in the ray so you're just comparing adjacent words here and the idea is that you build adjacent seas right build adjacency list so you can only determine one adjacency based on one comparison so one comparison means you at one thing one piece of information you cannot do anything after that single adjacency because everything after that is entirely like indeducible so you basically look at the two words then you have pointers to both of the words like a certain character starting from the beginning and you just keep advancing them until the letter is different at that point you simply point the letter of the first word to the second word in the graph as the adjacency there and then you just keep doing that for every single pair adjacent in the list and by that point you would have a graph that essentially you know has a bunch of adjacencies and it might happen that they overlap a lot and so you would perform a topological sort on that final graph and then I guess as to the fact that there could be invalid dictionaries is that maybe a topological sort is not possible in the graph, so example, maybe there's a cycle in the graph right? I think that would make the most sense if there's a cycle in the graph it's not possible and that's when you would return false or the empty string. I think that is the core of this problem and I think I actually should just do it like that should be pretty simple. I don't know, though let's see.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
