    {
      "id": "H2fSwhf1cctFEm_JO93Ju",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 613.2157581015897,
      "y": 516.966396106662,
      "width": 399.44946289062483,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0d",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 578081844,
      "version": 514,
      "versionNonce": 1932609292,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783052335792,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "So I think in this case another rule, we\ncan state to have an invalid dictionary\nas if the previous word is longer than\nthe next word or like I guess when the\npointer arrives at the end of the next\nword right where the previous word is\nstill not finished or something like that\nthat would also immediately break the\nprogram and just cause us too early\nshort circuit and return impossible",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "So I think in this case another rule, we can state to have an invalid dictionary as if the previous word is longer than the next word or like I guess when the pointer arrives at the end of the next word right where the previous word is still not finished or something like that that would also immediately break the program and just cause us too early short circuit and return impossible",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "CdXjd_qo_2RdwXorprAgG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1048.7620031040703,
      "y": 214.2196152825917,
      "width": 412.2066481927766,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0e",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2066331828,
      "version": 1308,
      "versionNonce": 1508888716,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783052466542,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "So now the main problem I'm thinking\nabout is the islands and the fact that\nthis isn't a fully connected graph so it's\nnot necessarily easy to do a topological\nsort because there are multiple little\nconnected graphs believe that with this\nproblem many times and I think in this\ncase, since it allows you to do any order,\nI think the best thing to do would just\nbe to kind of order these islands\nseparately as a separate problem and I\nsuppose the best way we could do that\nit's just by sorting an alphabetically for\nexample in the English alphabet is one\npossibility another is just I don't know\nanything arbitrary I mean, we could also\ndo like the actual individual words\nsortings as w whatever could work, but\nessentially the idea is that we put an\nentire island before the next so the last\nelement of one island is before the first\nelement of the other right and then we\njust psychologically sort them individually\nand then simply join everything together\ninto one string.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "So now the main problem I'm thinking about is the islands and the fact that this isn't a fully connected graph so it's not necessarily easy to do a topological sort because there are multiple little connected graphs believe that with this problem many times and I think in this case, since it allows you to do any order, I think the best thing to do would just be to kind of order these islands separately as a separate problem and I suppose the best way we could do that it's just by sorting an alphabetically for example in the English alphabet is one possibility another is just I don't know anything arbitrary I mean, we could also do like the actual individual words sortings as w whatever could work, but essentially the idea is that we put an entire island before the next so the last element of one island is before the first element of the other right and then we just psychologically sort them individually and then simply join everything together into one string.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
